# Project Sand — self-designed tasks (Import tab)

Experts can now build a task themselves and upload it through the **Import**
tab. It goes straight into automated review, and if it passes, on to manual
review. Everything in the workflow and authoring rules still applies —
difficulty, the rewritten instruction, the UI/layout claim, the test floor.

Start from the **downloaded task template**. Its layout is authoritative; do
not invent a structure.

## Start from a new primitive

Ship a **new domain primitive and a new UI shell** — not a noun-swap of a task
already approved or in review. The primitive is the thing the app is about
(a booking, a ledger entry, a patient chart, a build queue); the shell is how
it is laid out and navigated. Both new, or the package reads as a repeat.

Sketch the primitive, the scenario, and the defect families before writing any
code. Prefer bugs and layout failures grounded in real cases where a capable
model struggled, rather than synthetic complexity. Rebuilding after a "too
similar" verdict costs the whole package.

## Package shape

- `instruction.md` — the only prompt the solving agent sees.
- `environment/` — the application, its `Dockerfile`, and runtime files.
  Problem images live in `environment/problem_assets/`.
- `tests/` — the verifier, plus the runner and parser the template ships.
- `solution/` — the reference patch, applied by `solution/solve.sh`.
- Modern downloaded import templates have **no root `config.json`**. Do not
  invent one. Wire `base_commit`, `fail_to_pass_count`, and
  `pass_to_pass_count` in `task.toml`; tagged titles must be unique and parser
  counts must cover the complete suite. A future fresh template remains
  authoritative if its shape changes.

## Hard requirements before upload

- **The app must launch.** A package that does not boot in its container fails
  automated review immediately.
- **Manually exercise both states.** Launch and interact with the app at
  `base_commit`, then apply the reference solution and repeat the same
  scenarios. Harbor proves verifier consistency, not product correctness.
- **The app must have visuals, and at least one image must be attached.** Add
  another broken image whenever one view does not visibly cover the next
  distinct visual defect.
- **The images must be load-bearing.** The task should not be solvable without
  looking at them; they carry the layout half of the contract.
- **The UI defect must be real and severe.** Overlapping controls, clipped
  text, an element outside its container, unreadable contrast — something a
  reviewer would call objectively bad, moving to objectively good. Cosmetic
  nudges do not qualify.
- **The app must be original code.** Uploads are checked against public code
  hosts and rejected on a match, so the application cannot come from a tutorial
  repo, a public starter, or any published project. Write it for the task.
- No secrets, credentials, or unrelated personal information in any file.
- One archive, `.zip` / `.tar.gz` / `.tgz`, up to 512 MB.

## The local gate — in this order

Easiness audit first, then nop, then oracle twice. Hardening rewrites tests, so
a green run taken before the audit proves nothing about the pack that ships.

```bash
harbor run -p ./my-task -a nop      # reward.txt must be 0
harbor run -p ./my-task -a oracle   # reward.txt must be 1
harbor run -p ./my-task -a oracle   # again — a suite that flakes once is broken
harbor view ./jobs                  # inspect stdout/stderr/parser output
```

`nop` applies nothing and runs `tests/test.sh`; every `fail_to_pass` test must
FAIL and every `pass_to_pass` test must PASS. A reward of `1` means the bug is
already fixed at `base_commit`, or the tests never touch it.

`oracle` applies `solution/solve.sh` first; every `fail_to_pass` ∪
`pass_to_pass` test must PASS. A reward of `0` means one of three things: the
patch did not apply (its context lines no longer match `base_commit`), the
patch applied but the fix is incomplete, or `task.toml` counts/parser output do
not match the tagged suite. `harbor view ./jobs` distinguishes them.

Do not upload until nop is 0 and oracle is 1 on two consecutive runs.

## Archive hygiene

**Ship `.tar.gz`, not `.zip`.** A byte-identical `.zip` was rejected at intake
with only "the package did not pass import checks"; the same tree as `.tar.gz`
went straight through. The importer accepts both on paper — in practice use the
tarball.

**Do not deviate from the template's `task.toml`.** Raising `timeout_sec` or
`build_timeout_sec` above the shipped defaults is a plausible intake rejection,
as is shipping files the template does not have. Fill in the placeholder values
and change nothing else.

**Match the template's file list exactly.** No nested `.git` repository inside
`environment/app`, no `.dockerignore`, no `.gitignore`, no editor dotfiles. The
template ships a bare `app/.gitkeep`; your real source replaces it and nothing
else is added. `solve.sh` applies the patch with `git apply`, which works fine
outside a repository, so a committed history is not needed.

Name the wrapper directory after the task slug, the way the template's wrapper
matches its own name.

These fail before a human sees the work:

- **`HEAD` must equal `base_commit`.** Committing a stray fix after setting
  `base_commit` means oracle is patching a tree that no longer matches.
- **Unix file modes.** Executable bits intact on `solve.sh`, `test.sh`,
  `run_script.sh`, and `launch.sh`; no Windows or macOS metadata riding along.
- **Screenshots only under `environment/problem_assets/`.** Not at the root,
  not beside the source.
- **No extra root junk.** No `.DS_Store`, editor directories, `node_modules`,
  scratch notes, or leftover `UNVERIFIED.md` / `warning.md`.

Archive the task directory and upload it through the Import tab. The difficulty
probe runs on self-designed tasks exactly as it does on claimed ones.
